FabricNeoForge <p>In your <code>fabric.mod.json</code>, register the classes as entrypoints - <code>minefunkin</code> for the server side, <code>minefunkin-client</code> for the client side - and name the API version you wrote against:</p> <pre><code>{
  "entrypoints": {
    "minefunkin": [ "com.example.mymod.MyMineFunkinAddon" ],
    "minefunkin-client": [ "com.example.mymod.client.MyMineFunkinClientAddon" ]
  },
  "custom": {
    "minefunkin:api": "1.1"
  },
  "suggests": { "minefunkin": "*" }
}
</code></pre> <p>Without MineFunkin installed, Fabric never calls these entrypoints, so your mod needs no hard dependency.</p> <p>NeoForge has no entrypoints. Put <code>@MineFunkinPlugin</code> on each addon class instead (a public class with a no-argument constructor):</p> <pre><code>@MineFunkinPlugin
public final class MyMineFunkinAddon implements MineFunkinAddon { ... }

@MineFunkinPlugin
public final class MyMineFunkinClientAddon implements MineFunkinClientAddon { ... }
</code></pre> <p>and name the API version in your <code>neoforge.mods.toml</code>, with MineFunkin as an optional dependency:</p> <pre><code>[modproperties.mymod]
"minefunkin:api" = "1.1"

[[dependencies.mymod]]
modId = "minefunkin"
type = "optional"
versionRange = "[0.3.0,)"
ordering = "NONE"
side = "BOTH"
</code></pre> <p>MineFunkin finds the annotated classes in NeoForge's mod scan data (like JEI's <code>@JeiPlugin</code>) and only loads one once it knows the class implements the interface for the running side - so a <code>MineFunkinClientAddon</code> is never touched on a dedicated server.</p> <p><code>"minefunkin:api": "1.1"</code> is the API version you wrote against. MineFunkin serves it if the first number matches and the second isn't higher than its own (<code>1.0</code> and <code>1.1</code> both get 1.1). A mod asking for a version MineFunkin doesn't serve gets no handle, and the log says why.</p>

Chart event Song script Starts when the chart reaches it (<code>onEvent</code>) at the song's start (<code>onStart</code>) Good for one moment, or switching a mode on and off reacting to the whole song: hits, misses, beats Example Beat Flash (switched on and off by events) the showcase's Combo Meter <p>Lots of events are a bit of both: <code>onEvent</code> switches a mode on or off, and <code>onBeat</code> / <code>onFrame</code> do the work while it's on. Most of the Event Showcase is built like this:</p> <pre><code>params {
    on = bool { from = "value1", default = true, label = "On" },
}

local active = false

function onEvent(p)
    active = p.on
end

function onBeat(b)
    if not active then return end
    -- ... the effect, on every beat ...
end
</code></pre>
